# Line endings are decided here, not by whatever `core.autocrlf` each machine
# happens to have.
#
# Two problems this solves:
#
#   1. Phantom "modified" files. Git for Windows sets `core.autocrlf=true`
#      system-wide, so the working tree is checked out with CRLF. Any tool that
#      rewrites a file with LF then leaves it permanently modified in
#      `git status` while `git diff` reports nothing -- a confusing state to
#      land in mid-task. The Tauri CLI does exactly this to
#      src-tauri/Cargo.toml on `npm run tauri dev`.
#
#   2. A CRLF file reaching history. Nothing above stops a contributor whose
#      Git has `core.autocrlf=false` from committing CRLF, and this repo has
#      places that need LF either way: the shell script the sidecar uses, and
#      the Linux clippy/CI leg. Declaring the policy in-tree makes it hold for
#      every clone rather than depending on how each one is configured.
#
# The index is already LF throughout, so this changes no stored content -- only
# what Git writes into, and expects to find in, a working tree.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries: never line-ending-converted, never diffed as text. `text=auto`
# already classifies these correctly today; naming them means a future icon
# can't come down to a content guess.
*.png  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
